{"name":"Woodward Park","hex":"#755f4a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"woodward","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A warden of a wood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/woodward"},{"term":"park","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An area of land set aside for environment preservation or recreation.","A tract of ground kept in its natural state, about or adjacent to a residence, such as for the preservation of game, for walking, riding, or the like."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To bring (something such as a vehicle) to a halt or store in a specified place.","To defer (a matter) until a later date."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/park"}],"entries":[{"title":"Woodward Park (Fresno)","blocks":[{"text":"Woodward Park is a public park located in Fresno, California, abutting the San Joaquin River, opened in 1968. It is named after the late Ralph Woodward who bequeathed a portion of his estate to provide a regional park and bird sanctuary in Fresno. The park has a multi-use amphitheatre, an authentic Japanese Garden, disc golf course, BMX course, three children's playgrounds, a lake and trails that connect to the Lewis S. Eaton Trail."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Woodward_Park_Nima1.JPG/330px-Woodward_Park_Nima1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodward_Park_(Fresno)","wikidata":"Q8033578"},{"title":"Woodward Park (Tulsa)","blocks":[{"text":"Woodward Park is a 45-acre (18 ha) public park, botanical garden, and arboretum located between 21st Street and 24th Street east of South Peoria Avenue and west of South Rockford Street in Tulsa, Oklahoma, in the Midwestern United States. The park, named for Helen Woodward, the original property owner, was established in 1929, after a lengthy court suit over ownership."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Fall_in_Woodward_Park%2C_Tulsa%2C_OK.jpg/330px-Fall_in_Woodward_Park%2C_Tulsa%2C_OK.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodward_Park_(Tulsa)","wikidata":"Q8033579"},{"title":"Park","blocks":[{"text":"A park is an area of natural, semi-natural or planted space set aside for human enjoyment and recreation or for the protection of wildlife or natural habitats. Urban parks are green spaces set aside for recreation inside towns and cities. National parks and country parks are green spaces used for recreation in the countryside. State parks and provincial parks are administered by sub-national government states and agencies. Parks may consist of grassy areas, rocks, soil and trees, but may also contain buildings and other artifacts such as monuments, fountains or playground structures. Many parks have fields for playing sports such as baseball and football, and paved areas for games such as basketball. Many parks have trails for walking, biking and other activities. Some parks are built adjacent to bodies of water or watercourses and may comprise a beach or boat dock area. Urban parks often have benches for sitting and may contain picnic tables and barbecue grills."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Halleyparknovember_b_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Halleyparknovember_b_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park","wikidata":"Q22698"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Shinzen_Japanese_Garden_-_Flickr_-_mepp.jpg/500px-Shinzen_Japanese_Garden_-_Flickr_-_mepp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Shinzen_Japanese_Garden_-_Flickr_-_mepp.jpg/500px-Shinzen_Japanese_Garden_-_Flickr_-_mepp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shinzen Garden with Fall colors","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShinzen_Japanese_Garden_-_Flickr_-_mepp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Shinzen_Friendship_Garden_in_Fresno%2C_California.jpg/500px-Shinzen_Friendship_Garden_in_Fresno%2C_California.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Shinzen_Friendship_Garden_in_Fresno%2C_California.jpg/1280px-Shinzen_Friendship_Garden_in_Fresno%2C_California.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Shinzen_Friendship_Garden_in_Fresno%2C_California.jpg/500px-Shinzen_Friendship_Garden_in_Fresno%2C_California.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A stream running through the Fall Section of the Shinzen Garden","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShinzen_Friendship_Garden_in_Fresno%2C_California.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Granville_Grove_2022.jpg/500px-Granville_Grove_2022.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Granville_Grove_2022.jpg/1280px-Granville_Grove_2022.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Granville_Grove_2022.jpg/500px-Granville_Grove_2022.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The connected Lewis S. Eaton trail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGranville_Grove_2022.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Woodward_Park_en_Fresno%2C_California_%2833954896795%29.jpg/500px-Woodward_Park_en_Fresno%2C_California_%2833954896795%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign